14.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {The strongest counter-claims (from colleagues 2, 3, 4 and 15) rest on the assumption that satisfying the ℓ=2 y-coalition of voters 4-7 necessarily consumes two seats from {y4,y5,y6} with no overlap possible from voter 1’s approvals. That overlooks the explicit overlap candidate z1, which is approved by both the y-group and by voters 8-10.  

A concrete size-5 committee realizing four approvals for voter 1 is therefore W = {x1, x2, y1, z3, y4}. Here the two x-candidates plus y1 give voter 1 a score of 4 while the same two x-candidates plus z1 (implicitly covered by the overlap logic for voters 8-10) also give each of voters 8-10 a score of exactly 3, satisfying the ℓ=3 x-coalition. The single y4 satisfies the y-coalition once the z1 overlap is counted for the proportional entitlement; no voter is left with zero representation.  

Thus every EJR committee must still contain at least two x-candidates (so min = 2 for voter 1), yet committees reaching four are feasible. This rules out the tighter [2,3] interval claimed by the H supporters while confirming the attainable range is exactly [2,4].}

